  "research_next": "compass_interval",
  "aim": {
    "cw": "Break the Italian camp line at Nibeiwa, take Sidi Barrani, and cut the coast road towards Buq Buq.",
    "axis": "Hold Sidi Barrani and keep the westward coast road open for the Tenth Army."
  },
  "about": "Trial operational reconstruction of 9–16 December 1940 on the game's existing 10 km map, one day per turn. Nibeiwa, the nearby Tummar camps and Sidi Barrani can receive separate daily orders. Tummar West/East are represented by a sector north of Nibeiwa, not separate named places. The 7th RTR's approximately 50 infantry tanks and the named formations follow official accounts; most formation strengths, stocks, local hexes and the air/sea percentages are explicit game estimates requiring further research. Green experience and 60 opening cohesion for the forward Italian camps are gameplay proxies for the documented surprise and weak command response, not measured unit readiness. The 4th Indian Division withdraws at the end of 14 December, the battlefield departure date given in the Australian official history; 16th British Brigade and 7th RTR remain. Brigade 16 is linked to Western Desert Force HQ from the start so it can remain supplied after the division departs. The approach march of 7–8 December is outside this opening. The dated assaults and supply are still under review; this is not a validated historical simulation. Tobruk’s pre-existing Italian XXII Corps and Sirte garrison are carried from this chapter so a later campaign handoff never spawns defenders retroactively. The 8,000/6,400 men, 72 guns, and town fort level are game estimates; they are not a reconstruction of the roughly 25,000-man, 220-gun January garrison.",
  "sources": {
    "W": "Wavell, Western Desert operations despatch, London Gazette 37628, sections 10–21: plan, forces, opening, Nibeiwa and Sidi Barrani",
    "P": "Playfair et al., The Mediterranean and Middle East, vol. I, chapter XIV: positions, supply preparation, assault sequence and withdrawal of 4th Indian Division",
    "A": "Australian official history, To Benghazi, chapter 6: Commonwealth formations and armoured screening task",
    "E": "Game estimate: individual-unit strength, exact 10 km placement, opening stock or supply setting not verified by the cited account",
    "T": "Australian official history, To Benghazi, chapter 9: XXII Corps, 61st Sirte and Tobruk garrison; approximately 25,000 men and 220 guns estimated 15 January"
  },
